1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,888
Time to talk to a model from Python.

2
00:00:03,109 --> 00:00:08,915
In this lesson you'll set up an API key
safely, install the official Anthropic SDK,

3
00:00:08,915 --> 00:00:14,792
send your first message, and then take
the response apart, because every field in it

4
00:00:14,792 --> 00:00:16,891
matters when you build agents.

5
00:00:18,191 --> 00:00:20,930
You'll create and store an API key safely.

6
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:24,310
Install the official Anthropic Python SDK.

7
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:27,191
Send a request with messages dot create.

8
00:00:27,541 --> 00:00:32,818
And read the response: its content
blocks, its stop reason, and its token usage.

9
00:00:34,118 --> 00:00:37,183
First, create an API
key in the Claude Console.

10
00:00:37,403 --> 00:00:42,011
Note that API usage is billed
separately from a Claude chat subscription.

11
00:00:42,361 --> 00:00:47,212
Store it in an environment
variable called ANTHROPIC API KEY.

12
00:00:47,432 --> 00:00:52,923
The SDK reads that variable
automatically, so the key never appears in your code.

13
00:00:53,273 --> 00:00:57,735
Never paste a key into code, a
chat, a screenshot, or a git commit.

14
00:00:57,955 --> 00:01:02,890
If a key ever leaks, delete it in the
Console straight away and create a new one.

15
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:04,228
No key yet?

16
00:01:04,448 --> 00:01:05,441
No problem.

17
00:01:05,661 --> 00:01:11,358
Everything in this module also runs in
offline mode, which I'll explain in a moment.

18
00:01:12,658 --> 00:01:15,840
As always, start with a
fresh virtual environment.

19
00:01:16,190 --> 00:01:18,497
Then install the anthropic package.

20
00:01:18,717 --> 00:01:24,298
I'm pinning version 2 point 3 point 0,
the version this course was recorded with.

21
00:01:24,518 --> 00:01:30,671
It brings its own HTTP library and
pydantic, which we'll use for structured output.

22
00:01:31,021 --> 00:01:32,139
And set your key.

23
00:01:32,359 --> 00:01:38,180
On Windows PowerShell, use dollar
env colon ANTHROPIC API KEY instead.

24
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:42,330
Or keep it in a dot env
file, as you learned in Module 1.

25
00:01:43,630 --> 00:01:45,488
An honest note about the demos.

26
00:01:45,708 --> 00:01:50,990
So that every demo is free and
repeatable, and so you can follow along without a

27
00:01:50,990 --> 00:01:56,207
key, the replies you'll see come from
a small fake API built into this module's

28
00:01:56,207 --> 00:01:56,735
project.

29
00:01:56,955 --> 00:02:03,552
It speaks the same protocol as the real
API, so the real SDK code runs unchanged,

30
00:02:03,552 --> 00:02:06,891
including streaming, errors, and retries.

31
00:02:07,111 --> 00:02:11,706
But the replies are placeholders,
clearly marked offline demo reply.

32
00:02:11,926 --> 00:02:13,477
They are not model output.

33
00:02:13,697 --> 00:02:17,399
With your key set, exactly
the same code talks to Claude.

34
00:02:18,699 --> 00:02:24,142
In your own projects, you create a
client with anthropic dot Anthropic, which reads

35
00:02:24,142 --> 00:02:26,044
the key from the environment.

36
00:02:26,264 --> 00:02:31,277
Our lesson helper does the same when a
key is set, and uses the offline fake when it

37
00:02:31,277 --> 00:02:31,635
isn't.

38
00:02:31,985 --> 00:02:34,434
Messages dot create sends a request.

39
00:02:34,654 --> 00:02:40,609
Three arguments are required: the model
ID, max tokens, the longest reply you'll

40
00:02:40,609 --> 00:02:43,066
accept, and the list of messages.

41
00:02:43,286 --> 00:02:48,862
Each message is a dictionary with a role
and content, exactly the shape you built in

42
00:02:48,862 --> 00:02:49,460
Module 1.

43
00:02:49,810 --> 00:02:52,270
The reply's content is a list of blocks.

44
00:02:52,490 --> 00:02:55,742
Here there's a single text
block, so we print its text.

45
00:02:56,092 --> 00:03:01,183
And we print two more fields: why the
model stopped, and how many tokens went in and

46
00:03:01,183 --> 00:03:01,426
out.

47
00:03:02,726 --> 00:03:04,809
Here's the run, in offline mode.

48
00:03:05,029 --> 00:03:10,459
A placeholder reply, a stop reason of
end turn, which means the model finished

49
00:03:10,459 --> 00:03:12,686
naturally, and the token counts.

50
00:03:12,906 --> 00:03:16,540
With a real key you'd see a
real one-sentence answer here.

51
00:03:17,840 --> 00:03:21,406
Let's explore the response
object in the interactive prompt.

52
00:03:21,756 --> 00:03:27,157
Every response has an ID, which you
include when reporting problems, the model that

53
00:03:27,157 --> 00:03:29,369
answered, and the role, assistant.

54
00:03:29,589 --> 00:03:31,785
Content is a list of typed blocks.

55
00:03:32,135 --> 00:03:34,191
The stop reason and the usage.

56
00:03:34,411 --> 00:03:38,731
Usage is what you are billed for,
so production code always records it.

57
00:03:38,951 --> 00:03:40,930
We'll do exactly that in the project.

58
00:03:42,230 --> 00:03:43,653
Why is content a list?

59
00:03:43,873 --> 00:03:46,868
Because a reply can
contain different kinds of block.

60
00:03:47,088 --> 00:03:49,087
Text blocks hold the written answer.

61
00:03:49,437 --> 00:03:51,890
Thinking blocks can
hold the model's reasoning.

62
00:03:52,110 --> 00:03:56,657
On current models it's hidden by
default, but the block may still be there.

63
00:03:57,007 --> 00:04:02,413
And tool use blocks are requests to run
one of your tools, which is how agents act.

64
00:04:02,633 --> 00:04:04,811
You'll handle those in Module 5.

65
00:04:05,161 --> 00:04:08,803
So robust code never
assumes the first block is text.

66
00:04:09,023 --> 00:04:12,526
It checks each block's
type, as the next example does.

67
00:04:13,826 --> 00:04:19,078
The system parameter gives the model its
role and rules for the whole conversation.

68
00:04:19,298 --> 00:04:21,402
It sits outside the messages list.

69
00:04:21,622 --> 00:04:25,621
Here: be a patient tutor, and
answer in at most two sentences.

70
00:04:25,971 --> 00:04:30,961
And here's the robust way to read the
answer: loop over the blocks and only print

71
00:04:30,961 --> 00:04:31,700
text blocks.

72
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,601
In offline mode the placeholder
can't follow the instructions, of course.

73
00:04:37,821 --> 00:04:40,444
A real model would
answer in two sentences.

74
00:04:40,664 --> 00:04:44,643
Prompt design gets a whole
module of its own, Module 3.

75
00:04:45,943 --> 00:04:46,751
Quick check.

76
00:04:46,971 --> 00:04:50,809
Which three arguments does
messages dot create require?

77
00:04:56,509 --> 00:04:58,842
Model, max tokens, and messages.

78
00:04:59,062 --> 00:05:03,136
The key comes from the
environment, and the system prompt is optional.

79
00:05:04,436 --> 00:05:08,733
API keys live in an
environment variable, never in your code.

80
00:05:09,083 --> 00:05:13,630
Messages dot create needs a
model, max tokens, and messages.

81
00:05:13,980 --> 00:05:18,657
The response gives you content
blocks, a stop reason, and token usage.

82
00:05:19,007 --> 00:05:21,672
And the system prompt
sets the role and the rules.

83
00:05:22,022 --> 00:05:24,001
Next lesson: conversations.

84
00:05:24,221 --> 00:05:29,036
You'll see exactly why the model
forgets, and how to manage message history

85
00:05:29,036 --> 00:05:29,614
properly.
